// The one door the whole foreign world enters through (`spec/exceptions.md`
// section 6). JavaScript may throw any value at all — a `TypeError`, a bare
// string, `null`, a function — and every one of them arrives in Hexagon as a
// `JsError` carrying the raw thrown value.
//
// The exception lives in a module named for itself because its two accessors
// are spelled through that module: `JsError.message` and `JsError.stack` are
// what FFI Part 11 section 7 writes, and a qualified spelling needs a module
// addressable under the name (Modules section 3.3). The same reason seats
// `JsKind`, `JsPathSegment` and `JsConversionReason` in modules of their own.
//
// **The wrapping is virtual, and only in emission** (section 6.2). Nothing
// about the declaration below is unusual — it is an ordinary exception with an
// ordinary payload — and its typing and surface behaviour are ordinary too.
// What the emitter does with it is the special case: a `JsError(e)` catch arm
// *is* the foreign branch of the two-stage discrimination and binds the raw
// thrown value with nothing allocated, and `throw(JsError(e))` unwraps to
// `throw e`, so the rethrow-after-inspection idiom preserves the original
// error's identity and its stack. Only the exotic path — constructing a
// `JsError` and throwing it later — materialises the branded wrapper, which is
// section 6.2's recorded and accepted residue.
//
// The accessors are **total and conservative** (Part 11 section 7): every
// thrown value has an answer, no value's `toString` is ever invoked, and a
// property read that throws is swallowed rather than propagated — an accessor
// whose purpose is describing one failure must not manufacture a second.

(** A value JavaScript threw that Hexagon did not. `error` is that value,
    unchanged and unclassified: JavaScript permits throwing anything, so no
    structure is claimed about it and no decoding is attempted. Interrogate it
    with `JsError.message` and `JsError.stack`, or with the ordinary `JsValue`
    decoders when you want more than those two answer.

    A `JsError(e)` catch arm allocates nothing — it is where a foreign
    throwable arrives — and rethrowing one throws the original object, stack
    intact. *)
export exception JsError(error: JsValue)

// The three crossings, and only the three. Each is one JavaScript expression
// Hexagon has no spelling for, and each is unexported beneath the ordinary
// Hexagon that earns it (`spec/intrinsics.md` section 3.2): the guarded read
// has to be JavaScript because Hexagon has no way to catch a getter, and the
// safe rendering has to be JavaScript because a `Symbol` and `undefined` have
// no Hexagon decoder at all.
extern from "hex:intrinsic"
    // One property read of `.message`, guarded: a getter or proxy trap that
    // throws yields `undefined` here rather than propagating, and an absent
    // property yields `undefined` because that is what reading one gives. What
    // comes back is the value as read, so the string verdict below is ordinary
    // Hexagon over it and the value's own `toString` is never reached.
    fun jsErrorReadMessage as readMessage(error: JsValue): JsValue

    // The same single guarded read at `.stack`.
    fun jsErrorReadStack as readStack(error: JsValue): JsValue

    // The safe rendering of a value that bears no properties — the string
    // itself for a string, and `String(value)` for the other six kinds, which
    // is the one stringification that cannot throw on a `Symbol`. Asked only of
    // a value `kind` has already placed outside `Object` and `Function`, so it
    // never meets a `toString` of anyone's.
    fun jsErrorRender as render(value: JsValue): String

// The two verdicts, each ordinary Hexagon over one guarded read. A read that
// answered with a string is that string; anything else — a throw suppressed to
// `undefined`, an absent property, a non-string value — is the conservative
// fallback. `JsValue.toString` is the strict decoder, so nothing here coerces:
// a `.message` holding `42` is not `"42"`.

let readString(value: JsValue): String = match value.toString()
    Ok(text) => text
    Err(_) => ""

let readOption(value: JsValue): Option(String) = match value.toString()
    Ok(text) => Some(text)
    Err(_) => None

(** What `error` says about itself, always. A thrown object or function gets one
    guarded read of its `.message`, and a read that throws, is absent, or is not
    a string yields `""`. Anything else — a string, a number, a bigint, a
    boolean, a symbol, `undefined`, `null` — renders safely, so `throw "oops"`
    reads back as `"oops"`.

    The value's own `toString` is never invoked and no secondary failure ever
    escapes: this function describes a failure and must not manufacture one. *)
export let message(error: JsValue): String = match error.kind()
    JsKind.Object | JsKind.Function => readString(readMessage(error))
    _ => render(error)

(** `error`'s stack trace when it has one, `None` otherwise. A thrown object or
    function gets one guarded read of its `.stack` and answers `Some(trace)`
    only when that read succeeded with a string; a value that bears no
    properties has no stack to read and answers `None`.

    Conservative in the same way `message` is: a throwing read is swallowed, not
    propagated. The read is fresh on every call, because `.stack` may be an
    accessor. *)
export let stack(error: JsValue): Option(String) = match error.kind()
    JsKind.Object | JsKind.Function => readOption(readStack(error))
    _ => None
